First, I should factor each number separately. Let me start with 117. Hmm, 117... I know that 117 divided by 3 is 39 because 3 times 39 is 117. Then 39 can be divided by 3 again, giving 13. So 117 is 3 times 3 times 13. Therefore, the prime factors of 117 are 3 and 13. Wait, but since 3 is repeated, but we need different prime factors, so just 3 and 13.

Now, moving on to 119. Let me think. 119... I remember that 119 divided by 7 is 17. Because 7 times 17 is 119. Let me check: 7*10 is 70, 7*17 is 70 + 7*7 = 70 + 49 = 119. Yes, that's correct. So 119 factors into 7 and 17, both of which are prime numbers. So the prime factors of 119 are 7 and 17.

Now, multiplying 117 and 119 together, their prime factors would be the combination of the prime factors of each. So the prime factors of 117*119 would be 3, 13, 7, and 17. Let me check if any of these are duplicates. 3, 7, 13, 17—none of these are the same. So all four are distinct primes.

Therefore, the number of different prime factors is 4.

